WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:09.570
This is Daniela Rus,
Director of MIT CSAIL

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:11.880
and Deputy Dean of
Research at the Schwarzman

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.320
College of Computing.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:17.670
And I'm excited to welcome
you to the latest installment

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:20.340
of Hot Topics in Computing.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:23.610
Today, we'll be chatting
with an important thought

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:27.660
leader in technology,
and in particular

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:31.320
in the area of fake data.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.320
And this is
Professor Hany Farid.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.890
Professor Farid is now on
faculty at UC Berkeley,

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:41.550
but several years ago we
were colleagues and friends

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:43.140
in the computer
science department

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:44.970
at Dartmouth College.

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:48.780
Hany is also a member of
the broader MIT community,

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:50.730
as he was a postdoc here.

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:54.450
And I would like to introduce
Hany's postdoc advisor,

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:58.500
professor Ted Adelson, who
will introduce our speaker.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.300
Ted take it away, please.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:02.220
Thanks.

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:08.100
So it is my pleasure to
introduce Hany Farid.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:11.880
He has the distinction of being
one of the few people where

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:16.530
you can say he invented his own
field, which is digital imaging

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:18.900
forensics.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:22.200
I'm pleased to be able
to say I was there

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:28.004
when he invented this field,
but it had nothing to do myself.

00:01:28.004 --> 00:01:32.520
I was in my lab, working on
various more traditional image

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:36.840
processing topics and human
vision and computer vision.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:39.900
And one of the great
things about Hany,

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:42.060
at that time, and
still today, is

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:45.240
that he has very diverse
interests and skills.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.160
He's interested in
everything, and that

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:50.740
has turned out to be really
important for the work he's

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:51.240
done.

00:01:56.380 --> 00:02:03.100
So this is just a little
bit before 2000, 1998 or so.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:06.190
And people were
aware that the issue

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:09.520
of finding image
manipulation and fake images

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:13.400
was going to be an
important issue.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:17.640
Nobody really knew
what to do about it.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:19.350
Hany had some ideas.

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:23.570
I encouraged him to pursue them.

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:25.880
But when he told me
what he was trying to do

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:28.400
and how he was going to
do it, I explained to him

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:33.920
why it wouldn't work, and I
cited the relevant theorems

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:37.490
and offered counter examples.

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:39.830
But he persisted.

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:44.390
And finally, when he
demonstrated it worked,

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:47.750
I was convinced and
I was delighted.

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:52.520
And I also saw him give
a poster soon thereafter

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:56.870
at a conference, explaining
what he was doing.

00:02:56.870 --> 00:03:01.550
And a famous professor
came to the poster,

00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:04.110
started with a frown.

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:05.940
Explained to Hany
why it wouldn't work,

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:09.450
cited the theorems in the
math of why it wouldn't work.

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:11.400
And then continued
to do the poster,

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.110
and as he continued
in the poster,

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:15.990
he started smiling till the end.

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:18.630
He was like, wow,
that's really cool.

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:20.130
And occasionally,
at the conference,

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:21.990
I would go back to
see Hany's poster,

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:24.998
and I would watch as
the next person go

00:03:24.998 --> 00:03:27.540
through the same thing, staring
with a frown and ending with,

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:30.540
oh, wow, that's really cool.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:32.490
And so this is,
I think, typified

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:34.770
what he's done
through the years,

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:40.560
is time and again, through
his broad interests and skills

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:43.290
and creativity,
constantly found ways

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:45.750
to do things that everyone
thought was impossible,

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:49.590
and everyone could
prove was impossible.

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:54.100
And he continues to do
that today, and do it

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:57.300
in so many interesting ways.

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:59.250
And as we all know,
these problems

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:01.840
have become really
critical today.

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:04.200
So it will be very
interesting to hear

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:07.870
what he is working on now.

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:09.110
Take it away.

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:09.610
Thanks, Ted.

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:11.298
It's great seeing
you and Daniela.

00:04:11.298 --> 00:04:13.090
Wonderful to see you
after all these years.

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:17.329
I'm only sorry
it's not in person.

00:04:17.329 --> 00:04:18.910
So I'm going to
talk a little bit

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:21.350
about some of the most recent
work that we've been doing.

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:23.050
So hopefully you have
me in full screen.

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:25.930
I think you're in
full screen now.

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:28.900
And the work I'm going to talk
about for most of the talk

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:32.800
is that of Shruti Agarwal,
who's a really terrific PhD

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:33.670
student in my lab.

00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:34.630
Graduating next year.

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:36.160
She's going to be on
the job market soon,

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:37.577
so everybody should
pay attention.

00:04:37.577 --> 00:04:39.708
She is really, really wonderful.

00:04:39.708 --> 00:04:41.500
We're going to talk
about deep fake videos.

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:42.708
You may have heard this term.

00:04:42.708 --> 00:04:44.270
I'll define it for
you in a minute.

00:04:44.270 --> 00:04:47.620
But let me just start with the
obvious, and where Ted started,

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:51.020
is that image
manipulation is not new.

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:53.350
I mean, as long as we've
been taking photographs,

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:54.910
we've been manipulating
photographs.

00:04:54.910 --> 00:04:58.060
This, of course, is a
photograph of Stalin.

00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:01.840
The gentleman to Stalin's
left had fallen out of favor

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:05.530
and Stalin apparently
thought that he could just

00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:10.030
wipe this person out of
history by airbrushing him out

00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:11.210
of the photo.

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:13.190
And so how did they do this?

00:05:13.190 --> 00:05:14.770
Well, this is
obviously pre-digital

00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:16.750
and so this is done
in the darkroom.

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:19.570
You take the negative and
you literally paint over,

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:24.280
and then you reimage and
you can alter a photograph.

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:25.910
And Stalin wasn't
alone, by the way.

00:05:25.910 --> 00:05:27.243
You can look throughout history.

00:05:27.243 --> 00:05:28.930
You see evidence of
photo manipulation.

00:05:28.930 --> 00:05:30.430
And one of the
interesting questions

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:33.070
you want to ask
yourself is, well, why?

00:05:33.070 --> 00:05:34.640
Why did Stalin go
to the trouble?

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:37.120
Why not just knock the guy
off and be done with it?

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:40.390
And I posit that it's
because people like Stalin

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:44.290
understood the power
of photography.

00:05:44.290 --> 00:05:46.270
They understood the
power of visual imagery,

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:50.062
which is that if you can change
images, you can change history.

00:05:50.062 --> 00:05:51.520
And while the
underlying techniques

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:54.580
from manipulating images
have changed dramatically

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:58.750
over the decades, that
fundamental concept,

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:00.580
that there is power
in visual imagery,

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:02.650
I don't think has changed.

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:05.350
Now, fast forward to
the digital age and we

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:07.870
can do lots of fun things
with digital images

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:09.250
in things like Photoshop.

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:11.440
So relatively easy
to create the photo

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:13.900
like this, where
Trump's face was spliced

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:16.300
on to the body of a child
that former President

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:17.110
Obama is holding.

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:20.000
And we've been able probably
to do this for about 10,

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:21.350
15, 20 years.

00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:24.520
And as Ted was saying, when
I was working with Ted--

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:28.460
two, by the way, of the
best years of my career--

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:30.460
Photoshop was really
in its infancy.

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:32.350
Film still dominated
the landscape.

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:34.690
And I remember Ted and
I sitting in his office,

00:06:34.690 --> 00:06:38.980
looking at a graph
showing the sales of film

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:40.810
cameras and digital cameras.

00:06:40.810 --> 00:06:44.030
And just around the
time in the late '90s,

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:45.980
early 2000s was
the tipping point.

00:06:45.980 --> 00:06:49.090
It was the 50% mark where
film was on the way down

00:06:49.090 --> 00:06:50.480
and digital was on the way up.

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:52.290
And that's when I started
thinking about, well,

00:06:52.290 --> 00:06:54.623
what happens when we enter a
landscape and everything is

00:06:54.623 --> 00:06:58.330
digital and you can start
manipulating digital media?

00:06:58.330 --> 00:07:02.890
And that sort of led on
to a two-decade question

00:07:02.890 --> 00:07:05.290
of authenticating digital media.

00:07:05.290 --> 00:07:08.500
And what happened over that two
decades is for the first decade

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:11.650
or so, it was really
digital cameras, Photoshop,

00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:13.810
and things like this.

00:07:13.810 --> 00:07:16.570
And I remember talking to Ted
about this many years ago,

00:07:16.570 --> 00:07:17.500
maybe five years ago.

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:20.320
And Ted was saying, you know,
this is a great, little field.

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:23.410
It's a niche field.

00:07:23.410 --> 00:07:24.530
It's important.

00:07:24.530 --> 00:07:26.560
But it was small
and it was true,

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:30.640
but something weird has happened
over the last five years

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:34.090
with the misinformation
apocalypse that

00:07:34.090 --> 00:07:37.030
is the internet now and
the role of fake images

00:07:37.030 --> 00:07:39.160
and fake videos
and fake content.

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:41.615
And suddenly, what seemed to
be a relatively small field

00:07:41.615 --> 00:07:43.240
has become much, much
larger, and we'll

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:44.657
talk a little bit
more about where

00:07:44.657 --> 00:07:48.740
I see that in the coming years.

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:51.688
But in particular, what
has happened most recently

00:07:51.688 --> 00:07:53.230
in the last few
years is that we have

00:07:53.230 --> 00:07:56.140
gone from manipulating
things in the dark room

00:07:56.140 --> 00:07:58.480
to simple manipulations
in Photoshop

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:02.230
to full-blown synthesis
using advances in machine

00:08:02.230 --> 00:08:03.710
learning and big data.

00:08:03.710 --> 00:08:06.260
So we're going to talk
about this, in particular.

00:08:06.260 --> 00:08:10.180
So if you navigate to
thispersondoesnotexist.com,

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:13.040
you will be presented with
a single image of a person.

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:16.760
These are six images that I
grabbed off of that website.

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:18.560
And these are people
who don't exist.

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:23.285
They are completely full-blown
synthesized by deep learning,

00:08:23.285 --> 00:08:25.160
in particular generative
adversarial network,

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:27.640
which I'll talk about in
more detail in a minute.

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:30.880
Unlike CGI, unlike
computer-generated imagery,

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:32.919
where you have a 3D model
and a virtual camera

00:08:32.919 --> 00:08:34.900
and virtual light
and texture and hair

00:08:34.900 --> 00:08:37.794
and highly, highly
detailed, that's not this.

00:08:37.794 --> 00:08:41.020
This is a purely data-driven,
image-based technique

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:44.260
for synthesizing images
of people who don't exist.

00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:47.710
And that idea of
computer-synthesizing images

00:08:47.710 --> 00:08:52.780
or video or audio is what
is broadly called deep fake.

00:08:52.780 --> 00:08:55.630
So let's talk about how
these are made very quickly.

00:08:55.630 --> 00:08:57.430
It's a pretty simple idea.

00:08:57.430 --> 00:09:01.570
Your job is to generate
an image with a face

00:09:01.570 --> 00:09:03.640
of a person who doesn't exist.

00:09:03.640 --> 00:09:05.693
And the way we do, that
is we have a generator.

00:09:05.693 --> 00:09:07.360
The generator job is
to create an image.

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:09.130
They start by generating
a random image,

00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:12.880
slap down a bunch of pixels on
whatever resolution you want.

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:15.880
Hand that image over
to a discriminator.

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:19.210
That discriminator
has access to a bunch

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:22.420
of faces of actual people,
scraped from the internet,

00:09:22.420 --> 00:09:24.520
some database, whatever it is.

00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:26.320
And the discriminator's
job is to ask,

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:30.370
can I discriminate this image,
handed by the generator,

00:09:30.370 --> 00:09:32.600
from these images that are
known to be real people?

00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:34.240
And of course, that
would be a trivial task

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:35.698
to do, in this
case, because that's

00:09:35.698 --> 00:09:36.913
a bunch of random pixels.

00:09:36.913 --> 00:09:38.080
Loops back to the generator.

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:39.790
Says, nope, it's not a person.

00:09:39.790 --> 00:09:41.080
Keep trying again.

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:43.060
And eventually, after
enough iterations,

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:45.700
the generator will eventually
make an image of a person.

00:09:45.700 --> 00:09:47.750
That person will be sent
to the discriminator.

00:09:47.750 --> 00:09:50.440
And the discriminator will
say, yup, looks like a person.

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:54.190
So StyleGAN, StyleGAN2, other
techniques for doing this.

00:09:54.190 --> 00:09:57.910
And they create highly, highly
realistic images of people.

00:09:57.910 --> 00:09:59.950
And now we're even
starting to have control,

00:09:59.950 --> 00:10:01.960
where you can play with
the gender and the race

00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:04.630
and the age and the features
on it so it's not just

00:10:04.630 --> 00:10:08.440
a random person, but you start
to have some controls on it

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:11.440
the way you would
in a CGI world.

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:13.842
Now, that's one type of
deep fake, a static image.

00:10:13.842 --> 00:10:16.300
We'll talk a little bit about
where that type of technology

00:10:16.300 --> 00:10:19.290
is being weaponized,
because that's

00:10:19.290 --> 00:10:21.310
what the bulk of what
we're going to talk about,

00:10:21.310 --> 00:10:24.670
is our deep fake videos,
is a deep fake video.

00:10:24.670 --> 00:10:27.817
So now imagine taking that
same generative adversarial

00:10:27.817 --> 00:10:29.650
network-- generative
because you're building

00:10:29.650 --> 00:10:31.942
something, adversarial because
you're pitting these two

00:10:31.942 --> 00:10:33.275
systems against each other.

00:10:33.275 --> 00:10:34.900
And of course,
underneath each of these

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:37.288
is a deep neural network.

00:10:37.288 --> 00:10:39.580
So now imagine we're going
to do something a little bit

00:10:39.580 --> 00:10:40.220
different.

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:43.100
We're going to take this
image of Jennifer Lawrence,

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:46.570
the performer, and we
want to change her face

00:10:46.570 --> 00:10:49.180
to be that of Steve Buscemi.

00:10:49.180 --> 00:10:50.380
Why Steve Buscemi?

00:10:50.380 --> 00:10:51.760
I mean, why not?

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:53.500
Anybody it could have been.

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:56.000
So now what we're going
to do is the following.

00:10:56.000 --> 00:10:59.470
This generator has to change
her identity to his identity,

00:10:59.470 --> 00:11:01.510
and it does it by
modifying some pixels,

00:11:01.510 --> 00:11:03.220
handing it to the discriminator.

00:11:03.220 --> 00:11:06.730
The discriminator says, can
I discriminate that person

00:11:06.730 --> 00:11:11.290
from the actor Steve Buscemi
while keeping the appearance

00:11:11.290 --> 00:11:12.740
of the image the same?

00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:14.560
So his head should be
down and to the left,

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:16.590
his eyes should be
slightly closed.

00:11:16.590 --> 00:11:18.628
The appearance of the
lighting and the texture

00:11:18.628 --> 00:11:19.420
should be the same.

00:11:19.420 --> 00:11:21.800
All I want to do
is change identity.

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:24.590
And again, you do this in a
very, very, very tight loop.

00:11:24.590 --> 00:11:26.770
And if you do that
over and over again,

00:11:26.770 --> 00:11:29.290
frame after frame
after frame, you

00:11:29.290 --> 00:11:30.890
get something that
looks like this.

00:11:30.890 --> 00:11:32.710
So original video is here.

00:11:32.710 --> 00:11:35.590
A face swap deep
fake is here, where

00:11:35.590 --> 00:11:39.478
Steve Buscemi has been mapped
onto Jennifer Lawrence's face.

00:11:39.478 --> 00:11:41.020
There's actually an
audio track here.

00:11:41.020 --> 00:11:43.310
I've just taken it out
so I can talk over it.

00:11:43.310 --> 00:11:44.488
So a couple of things.

00:11:44.488 --> 00:11:46.780
First of all, who would have
guessed that Steve Buscemi

00:11:46.780 --> 00:11:47.988
is such a good-looking woman?

00:11:47.988 --> 00:11:49.730
I mean, he looks fantastic here.

00:11:49.730 --> 00:11:53.018
And notice how seamless this is.

00:11:53.018 --> 00:11:54.310
The lighting looks really good.

00:11:54.310 --> 00:11:55.685
The expression
looks really good.

00:11:55.685 --> 00:11:56.710
The head movements.

00:11:56.710 --> 00:11:59.230
Everything is moving, just
on every single frame.

00:11:59.230 --> 00:12:02.290
All that has been done is we've
done a face swap deep fake,

00:12:02.290 --> 00:12:04.313
frame after frame after
frame after frame.

00:12:04.313 --> 00:12:06.230
By the way, if you haven't
seen some of these,

00:12:06.230 --> 00:12:08.350
there are really, really
good examples on YouTube.

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:11.140
My favorite one just
came out a few weeks ago.

00:12:11.140 --> 00:12:13.120
Sylvester Stallone and
Arnold Schwarzenegger

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:16.540
had been spliced into the
movie Stepbrother's, and it

00:12:16.540 --> 00:12:18.400
is very, very funny
and very well done.

00:12:18.400 --> 00:12:22.470
Slightly not safe for
work, but very well done.

00:12:22.470 --> 00:12:25.710
So this is one type
of deep fake, where

00:12:25.710 --> 00:12:27.660
we've replaced a
person's face, typically

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:32.125
from the eyebrows to the chin,
from one face to another.

00:12:32.125 --> 00:12:33.000
There are two others.

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:34.260
Let me just mention them
briefly because I'll

00:12:34.260 --> 00:12:35.550
talk about them later.

00:12:35.550 --> 00:12:37.440
There is a puppet
master deep fake.

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:40.260
So here what we do is we take
a single image of a person,

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:41.970
and then I am the puppet master.

00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:45.120
So as I sit in front of the
camera and move my eyebrows

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:48.150
and turn my head
and smile and talk,

00:12:48.150 --> 00:12:50.382
it animates that single image.

00:12:50.382 --> 00:12:51.840
There's a beautiful
work coming out

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:54.810
of the University of Southern
California from Hao Li's group

00:12:54.810 --> 00:12:56.280
doing that.

00:12:56.280 --> 00:12:58.320
A lip sync deep fake
is slightly different.

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:01.590
Here, we take an
original video and we

00:13:01.590 --> 00:13:04.800
have a new audio stream,
either synthesized

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:06.570
or just an impersonator.

00:13:06.570 --> 00:13:09.690
And all that we
synthesize is the mouth

00:13:09.690 --> 00:13:12.910
to be consistent with
a new audio track.

00:13:12.910 --> 00:13:15.510
So whether it's the
mouth, the entire head,

00:13:15.510 --> 00:13:19.850
or just the face, each of these
deep fakes, what are we doing?

00:13:19.850 --> 00:13:23.310
We're trying to make somebody
say and do something that they

00:13:23.310 --> 00:13:26.370
didn't, either for
comedic purposes,

00:13:26.370 --> 00:13:30.150
to interfere with a democracy,
or to commit large scale fraud,

00:13:30.150 --> 00:13:33.870
by giving Zuckerberg to say
that his profits are down by 20%

00:13:33.870 --> 00:13:35.370
and trying to move
the stock market.

00:13:38.935 --> 00:13:41.310
So the game we play here is
we don't create these things.

00:13:41.310 --> 00:13:42.480
We want to detect them.

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:44.010
Why do we want to detect them?

00:13:44.010 --> 00:13:46.800
Well, because we think that
these can be weaponized.

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:50.400
So where are we currently seeing
weaponization of deep fakes?

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:52.650
Single-- I don't think it
will shock you, sadly--

00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:55.470
single most common
use of deep fakes

00:13:55.470 --> 00:13:58.770
is in the creation of
nonconsensual pornography.

00:13:58.770 --> 00:14:00.750
A woman's likeness
is superimposed

00:14:00.750 --> 00:14:04.710
into sexually explicit
material, and that material

00:14:04.710 --> 00:14:05.790
is distributed online.

00:14:05.790 --> 00:14:08.230
This is probably the
most common form of it,

00:14:08.230 --> 00:14:12.300
and in fact, it is where the
term "deep fake" came from.

00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:15.120
Deep Fake is the handle
name of a Reddit user

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:16.950
who used this
technology to create

00:14:16.950 --> 00:14:19.890
the first nonconsensual
pornography.

00:14:19.890 --> 00:14:22.840
Big, big problem for
performers, famous people,

00:14:22.840 --> 00:14:24.960
but also a problem
for journalists,

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:27.750
human rights activists,
people who simply

00:14:27.750 --> 00:14:29.160
attract unwanted attention.

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:31.530
There have been examples
of very high profile

00:14:31.530 --> 00:14:33.930
cases of very powerful
women who have

00:14:33.930 --> 00:14:36.270
tried to speak truth to
power and have been harassed

00:14:36.270 --> 00:14:37.380
with this type of content.

00:14:37.380 --> 00:14:39.930
It is awful and many
of the big platforms

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:42.780
have not figured out, or are
not willing to figure out,

00:14:42.780 --> 00:14:45.300
how to contend with that.

00:14:45.300 --> 00:14:49.110
We are all living through
highly complex and intricate

00:14:49.110 --> 00:14:53.130
misinformation campaigns meant
to sow civil unrest, meant

00:14:53.130 --> 00:14:55.350
to interfere with
democratic elections,

00:14:55.350 --> 00:14:57.870
for the purposes of
trolling or for fraud.

00:14:57.870 --> 00:14:59.970
And these types of
images and video

00:14:59.970 --> 00:15:02.790
are simply adding
fuel to that fire.

00:15:02.790 --> 00:15:07.650
So for example, my favorite
example of the use of a face

00:15:07.650 --> 00:15:11.610
synthesized, using This Person
Does Not Exist, was last year,

00:15:11.610 --> 00:15:15.420
a 17-year-old kid in Florida was
bored during his holiday break

00:15:15.420 --> 00:15:18.930
and he created a fictional
candidate for Congress

00:15:18.930 --> 00:15:20.160
in Rhode Island.

00:15:20.160 --> 00:15:23.430
He got that candidate
approved by the election board

00:15:23.430 --> 00:15:26.430
and got an approved
Twitter account

00:15:26.430 --> 00:15:30.150
using a face of the candidate
that was synthesized using

00:15:30.150 --> 00:15:31.380
This Person Does Not Exist.

00:15:31.380 --> 00:15:33.570
He created a completely
fictional character

00:15:33.570 --> 00:15:36.870
that, as far as anybody
knew, actually existed.

00:15:36.870 --> 00:15:40.800
We've seen these types of faces
being used and misinformation

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:44.460
campaigns to create fictional
profiles on Facebook

00:15:44.460 --> 00:15:48.930
and LinkedIn and on Twitter to
create credibility as people

00:15:48.930 --> 00:15:50.715
create fraudulent accounts.

00:15:50.715 --> 00:15:53.460
There are entire websites
now, by the way, where

00:15:53.460 --> 00:15:55.688
you can pay somebody $1.99
to create any type of face

00:15:55.688 --> 00:15:58.230
that you want, or you can just
download it for free from This

00:15:58.230 --> 00:15:59.992
Person Does Not Exist.

00:15:59.992 --> 00:16:02.820
As Ted will tell you, I
got into this business

00:16:02.820 --> 00:16:04.770
because I was worried
about the courts.

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:07.128
So 20 years ago, we
didn't have the internet

00:16:07.128 --> 00:16:07.920
that we have today.

00:16:07.920 --> 00:16:09.628
We didn't have
nonconsensual pornography.

00:16:09.628 --> 00:16:12.000
We didn't have even the
concept of fake news the way

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:12.990
we do today.

00:16:12.990 --> 00:16:15.870
But we were worried about how
we introduce digital evidence

00:16:15.870 --> 00:16:19.170
into a court of law, and
this creates a big problem

00:16:19.170 --> 00:16:22.982
for how do we trust
the digital evidence,

00:16:22.982 --> 00:16:25.440
whether it's an image, an audio
recording, or a video being

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:27.560
introduced into a court of law?

00:16:27.560 --> 00:16:29.310
Obviously, on the
national security scale,

00:16:29.310 --> 00:16:31.950
we are constantly, on a
daily basis, being inundated

00:16:31.950 --> 00:16:34.800
with visual imagery from
around the world and places

00:16:34.800 --> 00:16:36.600
that we can't
physically be, and we

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:38.910
have to make things
that have huge impacts

00:16:38.910 --> 00:16:40.050
on the world around us.

00:16:40.050 --> 00:16:42.900
How do we do that when we
don't know if we can trust

00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:44.820
the visual imagery that we see?

00:16:44.820 --> 00:16:46.590
Fraud, I've already
mentioned it.

00:16:46.590 --> 00:16:48.840
I create a video of
Jeff Bezos saying,

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:52.512
Amazon stocks are down
10%, profits are down 10%.

00:16:52.512 --> 00:16:53.970
How long do you
think it'll take me

00:16:53.970 --> 00:16:56.910
to move the market a few billion
dollars before anybody figures

00:16:56.910 --> 00:16:58.755
out that that video is fake?

00:16:58.755 --> 00:17:00.630
We've already seen the
first example of this.

00:17:00.630 --> 00:17:06.960
A CEO lost $250 million,
or 250 million euros,

00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:10.349
because somebody had created
a synthetic voice over a phone

00:17:10.349 --> 00:17:12.599
call saying, wire
this amount of money

00:17:12.599 --> 00:17:14.229
per this person's approval.

00:17:14.229 --> 00:17:15.104
They wired the money.

00:17:15.104 --> 00:17:18.420
A quarter million was lost
from a synthetic voice.

00:17:18.420 --> 00:17:21.420
So all of these things
are either here today

00:17:21.420 --> 00:17:24.390
or we are probably going to
be seeing them in the coming

00:17:24.390 --> 00:17:27.060
years, and this gives us
a little bit of concern

00:17:27.060 --> 00:17:30.222
for how these types of deep
fakes can be weaponized.

00:17:30.222 --> 00:17:32.430
And this is what we want to
think a little bit about,

00:17:32.430 --> 00:17:35.548
is how do we detect these?

00:17:35.548 --> 00:17:37.340
So I'm going to talk
about some of the work

00:17:37.340 --> 00:17:39.257
that Shruti has been
doing on this front, just

00:17:39.257 --> 00:17:42.410
one of the techniques that
we've been developing.

00:17:42.410 --> 00:17:45.500
So I'm going to show you in the
next slide a series of clips

00:17:45.500 --> 00:17:47.180
of former President Obama.

00:17:47.180 --> 00:17:49.070
They are just a
short little two-

00:17:49.070 --> 00:17:52.880
to three-second clips
from his weekly address.

00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:55.317
I ripped out the audio
just so I can talk over it.

00:17:55.317 --> 00:17:57.650
So you're going to see a
little clip after a little clip

00:17:57.650 --> 00:17:58.760
after a little clip.

00:17:58.760 --> 00:18:02.060
And in each one of those clips,
he's saying, hi, everybody.

00:18:02.060 --> 00:18:02.720
Hi, everybody.

00:18:02.720 --> 00:18:05.840
It's the very beginning
of his weekly address.

00:18:05.840 --> 00:18:10.465
See if you notice
anything during the clips.

00:18:10.465 --> 00:18:12.840
So every one of these, again,
he's saying, hi, everybody.

00:18:12.840 --> 00:18:13.650
Hi, everybody.

00:18:13.650 --> 00:18:14.520
Hi, everybody.

00:18:14.520 --> 00:18:15.480
Hi, everybody.

00:18:15.480 --> 00:18:18.410
Hey, everybody.

00:18:18.410 --> 00:18:19.910
OK, so those are
all distinct clips.

00:18:19.910 --> 00:18:22.077
I could have done that for
another 20 minutes or so.

00:18:22.077 --> 00:18:25.280
And I don't know if you noticed
it, but every time he did it,

00:18:25.280 --> 00:18:29.390
there's this little tilt of
the head up and to the back.

00:18:29.390 --> 00:18:30.660
And you can see it here.

00:18:30.660 --> 00:18:31.425
He's coming down.

00:18:31.425 --> 00:18:33.050
So every time he says
hi, everybody, he

00:18:33.050 --> 00:18:34.260
says hi, everybody.

00:18:34.260 --> 00:18:36.200
And you can see it right
here on that slide.

00:18:36.200 --> 00:18:38.900
He purses his lips
ever so slightly

00:18:38.900 --> 00:18:40.070
and then he starts talking.

00:18:40.070 --> 00:18:42.290
Almost every single
weekly address

00:18:42.290 --> 00:18:44.270
he starts exactly the same way.

00:18:44.270 --> 00:18:46.220
When Shruti first
showed me this--

00:18:46.220 --> 00:18:48.470
this is actually a small
version of a longer version--

00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:50.037
I was sure she had
duplicate in it

00:18:50.037 --> 00:18:52.620
because it didn't seem like they
could have all been distinct.

00:18:52.620 --> 00:18:54.780
Those are all distinct
little videos.

00:18:54.780 --> 00:18:55.760
So what is that?

00:18:55.760 --> 00:18:57.650
It's a little
behavioral mannerism.

00:18:57.650 --> 00:18:59.210
It's a little tick.

00:18:59.210 --> 00:19:00.290
I have mine, by the way.

00:19:00.290 --> 00:19:01.790
I've been doing a
lot of Zoom talks.

00:19:01.790 --> 00:19:02.870
I've been noticing them.

00:19:02.870 --> 00:19:05.510
How I raise my eyebrows
when I emphasize.

00:19:05.510 --> 00:19:08.750
I tend to move forward when I
want to emphasize something.

00:19:08.750 --> 00:19:10.678
President Obama does
this thing where,

00:19:10.678 --> 00:19:12.470
when he says, hey,
everybody, he does this.

00:19:12.470 --> 00:19:13.370
He has other ones.

00:19:13.370 --> 00:19:15.390
Let me give you an
example of that.

00:19:15.390 --> 00:19:16.982
Here's my favorite one.

00:19:16.982 --> 00:19:18.690
So what I'm showing
you here is one frame

00:19:18.690 --> 00:19:21.090
from an authentic Obama video.

00:19:21.090 --> 00:19:23.100
And on this graph,
what I'm showing you,

00:19:23.100 --> 00:19:25.870
along the horizontal
axis, is time.

00:19:25.870 --> 00:19:27.900
This is about 10
seconds, so roughly let's

00:19:27.900 --> 00:19:30.150
call it 250 frames or so.

00:19:30.150 --> 00:19:32.820
And in blue, I'm
plotting how much

00:19:32.820 --> 00:19:36.510
Obama is rotating his head
around the horizontal axis,

00:19:36.510 --> 00:19:39.090
as if in the affirmative,
nodding in the affirmative.

00:19:39.090 --> 00:19:42.420
That's the blue graph
curve right there.

00:19:42.420 --> 00:19:44.700
The orange curve is how
much he is frowning,

00:19:44.700 --> 00:19:48.060
so the sides of his mouth
are turning downward.

00:19:48.060 --> 00:19:51.300
And this is averaged over
an hour or so with video.

00:19:51.300 --> 00:19:52.770
And what do you notice?

00:19:52.770 --> 00:19:54.300
They're correlated.

00:19:54.300 --> 00:19:56.550
So Obama has this
interesting mannerisms.

00:19:56.550 --> 00:19:59.190
And you can see it
now, when you go back

00:19:59.190 --> 00:20:01.140
and watch some of the
speeches, that when

00:20:01.140 --> 00:20:04.290
he delivers bad news, when
he's angry, when he's sad,

00:20:04.290 --> 00:20:06.760
when he's upset, he does this.

00:20:06.760 --> 00:20:09.250
And he frowns and there's a
slight tilting of the head

00:20:09.250 --> 00:20:11.260
downward when he speaks.

00:20:11.260 --> 00:20:14.890
It's very consistent and it's
a very interesting, again,

00:20:14.890 --> 00:20:15.870
behavioral mannerism.

00:20:15.870 --> 00:20:17.620
Why do I care about
behavioral mannerisms?

00:20:17.620 --> 00:20:19.270
We'll get to that in a minute.

00:20:19.270 --> 00:20:22.750
But now let's think about
a lip sync deep fake.

00:20:22.750 --> 00:20:24.220
So what was a lip
sync deep fake?

00:20:24.220 --> 00:20:27.070
I replaced his mouth
with a new mouth

00:20:27.070 --> 00:20:30.040
that has been synthesized,
saying something else

00:20:30.040 --> 00:20:32.260
while his head was
saying whatever it was

00:20:32.260 --> 00:20:34.720
saying in the original video.

00:20:34.720 --> 00:20:36.970
Same thing, time on
the horizontal axis.

00:20:36.970 --> 00:20:37.930
10 seconds.

00:20:37.930 --> 00:20:40.750
Blue is rotation of
the head, up and down.

00:20:40.750 --> 00:20:43.570
Orange is frowning.

00:20:43.570 --> 00:20:44.950
What's wrong here?

00:20:44.950 --> 00:20:45.970
They're decoupled.

00:20:45.970 --> 00:20:46.570
Why?

00:20:46.570 --> 00:20:48.340
Well, because the
head was saying what

00:20:48.340 --> 00:20:50.650
the head was saying
in the original video,

00:20:50.650 --> 00:20:53.500
but the mouth is saying, well,
whatever the mouth is saying

00:20:53.500 --> 00:20:55.752
in the synthesized audio.

00:20:55.752 --> 00:20:58.210
And although it looks all fine--
the mouth is doing what it

00:20:58.210 --> 00:21:00.585
should be doing and the head
is sort of doing something--

00:21:00.585 --> 00:21:03.655
I've carried this
chimera of sort.

00:21:03.655 --> 00:21:05.530
The head doesn't know
what the mouth is doing

00:21:05.530 --> 00:21:07.613
and the mouth doesn't know
what the head is doing,

00:21:07.613 --> 00:21:11.500
and I've disrupted that
behavioral mannerism.

00:21:11.500 --> 00:21:14.170
Now, that's Obama's.

00:21:14.170 --> 00:21:16.780
This guy has a different
behavioral mannerism.

00:21:16.780 --> 00:21:17.800
So same thing.

00:21:17.800 --> 00:21:19.240
Time, 10 seconds.

00:21:19.240 --> 00:21:22.395
In blue now is this chin
pucker that Trump does.

00:21:22.395 --> 00:21:23.770
I actually can't
do it very well.

00:21:23.770 --> 00:21:24.610
I don't know how he does it.

00:21:24.610 --> 00:21:26.235
He does this weird
thing with his chin,

00:21:26.235 --> 00:21:27.340
if you've seen him talk.

00:21:27.340 --> 00:21:30.730
And the orange going
up here is this thing

00:21:30.730 --> 00:21:33.100
he does with his
mouth when he talks.

00:21:33.100 --> 00:21:36.640
And what you notice here is
that they're decorrelated.

00:21:36.640 --> 00:21:39.512
So when he chin puckers,
his mouth is closed.

00:21:39.512 --> 00:21:41.470
When his mouth is open,
he doesn't chin pucker.

00:21:41.470 --> 00:21:42.880
Right there, correlated.

00:21:42.880 --> 00:21:47.440
All right, Alec Baldwin doing
Trump in Saturday Night Live.

00:21:47.440 --> 00:21:49.300
We created a face
swap deep fake.

00:21:49.300 --> 00:21:54.220
So that's Trump's face
slapped on to Alec Baldwin.

00:21:54.220 --> 00:21:55.960
What's going on
with Alec Baldwin?

00:21:55.960 --> 00:21:56.620
Ah.

00:21:56.620 --> 00:22:00.885
So he's got like the individual
characteristics of Trump, this

00:22:00.885 --> 00:22:04.660
and the mouth, but
he correlates them.

00:22:04.660 --> 00:22:08.950
So he's got that sort
of caricature of Trump,

00:22:08.950 --> 00:22:13.147
but he didn't get the dynamics
right or the correlation right.

00:22:13.147 --> 00:22:14.980
And so what we're going
to do is we're going

00:22:14.980 --> 00:22:17.110
to explore various people--

00:22:17.110 --> 00:22:21.370
Obama, Trump, Harris,
Biden, Warren.

00:22:21.370 --> 00:22:24.160
Pick your favorite candidate,
senator, president,

00:22:24.160 --> 00:22:26.505
prime minister, CEO,
whomever it is--

00:22:26.505 --> 00:22:28.630
and we're going to learn
what their mannerisms are,

00:22:28.630 --> 00:22:31.660
and we're going to try to
use that to distinguish them

00:22:31.660 --> 00:22:33.520
from impersonators.

00:22:33.520 --> 00:22:37.900
So fundamentally, we're not
really going after deep fakes.

00:22:37.900 --> 00:22:39.477
What I'm going after
is whether it's

00:22:39.477 --> 00:22:41.560
a face swap, whether it's
a lip sync, whether it's

00:22:41.560 --> 00:22:42.790
a puppet master.

00:22:42.790 --> 00:22:44.230
It's an impersonator.

00:22:44.230 --> 00:22:46.420
It is whoever is
doing these actions,

00:22:46.420 --> 00:22:49.150
either you've disrupted
the natural dynamics

00:22:49.150 --> 00:22:51.220
of the head, the
mouth, the expressions,

00:22:51.220 --> 00:22:52.460
or it's just not them.

00:22:52.460 --> 00:22:56.320
It's somebody else
driving the mannerisms.

00:22:56.320 --> 00:22:58.660
So here's what
we're going to do.

00:22:58.660 --> 00:23:01.510
I think I've already said that
with the lip sync deep fake,

00:23:01.510 --> 00:23:03.490
you disrupt what the
mouth and head are doing.

00:23:03.490 --> 00:23:05.140
They don't know
about each other.

00:23:05.140 --> 00:23:07.510
And for the face swap
and the puppet master,

00:23:07.510 --> 00:23:08.980
it's somebody else moving.

00:23:08.980 --> 00:23:10.990
It's somebody else's
mannerisms, even

00:23:10.990 --> 00:23:12.903
if it looks like somebody else.

00:23:12.903 --> 00:23:14.320
And we're going
to use that, we're

00:23:14.320 --> 00:23:17.590
going to try to exploit
that, as a soft biometric

00:23:17.590 --> 00:23:20.140
to distinguish real from fake.

00:23:20.140 --> 00:23:22.150
So here's what
we're going to do.

00:23:22.150 --> 00:23:24.790
Take a video, do
standard face tracking.

00:23:24.790 --> 00:23:30.790
So here, the blue box
gives me 3D orientation.

00:23:30.790 --> 00:23:32.900
The red dots that you
see around the face

00:23:32.900 --> 00:23:37.450
give me facial features,
from which I will extract

00:23:37.450 --> 00:23:40.180
18 so-called action units.

00:23:40.180 --> 00:23:42.190
So these tell me, are you
lifting your eyebrows?

00:23:42.190 --> 00:23:43.030
Are you frowning?

00:23:43.030 --> 00:23:44.260
Are you smiling?

00:23:44.260 --> 00:23:46.820
So these various
standard action units.

00:23:46.820 --> 00:23:48.760
So for every moment
in time, as I'm

00:23:48.760 --> 00:23:51.490
tracking that video over a
10-second window, roughly

00:23:51.490 --> 00:23:56.350
250 frames, I extract these
18 action units and two head

00:23:56.350 --> 00:24:01.060
rotations, rotation like
this and rotation like this.

00:24:01.060 --> 00:24:03.580
We ignore this rotation,
around the vertical axis,

00:24:03.580 --> 00:24:06.160
because politicians do
this a lot as they're

00:24:06.160 --> 00:24:09.700
scanning the audience
and it's not diagnostic.

00:24:09.700 --> 00:24:12.400
So we have 20
measurements that we make.

00:24:12.400 --> 00:24:16.270
That gives us 22 [? 2, ?]
20 times 19 divided by 2,

00:24:16.270 --> 00:24:20.500
190 pairs of expressions and
head movements, similar to what

00:24:20.500 --> 00:24:24.160
I showed you for Trump, similar
to what I showed you for Obama.

00:24:24.160 --> 00:24:27.730
Just look at the correlation,
or anti-correlation,

00:24:27.730 --> 00:24:29.510
of facial expressions.

00:24:29.510 --> 00:24:35.315
So every 10-second clip I
look at 190 correlations,

00:24:35.315 --> 00:24:36.940
and I get a single
number off from each

00:24:36.940 --> 00:24:38.770
of those 190 correlations.

00:24:38.770 --> 00:24:41.170
So every 10-second
clip is now a point

00:24:41.170 --> 00:24:45.190
in 190-dimensional space, which
of course we can't visualize.

00:24:45.190 --> 00:24:49.630
I'm going to slam that into
this two-dimensional space here.

00:24:49.630 --> 00:24:53.140
And every data point here
is a 10-second video clip

00:24:53.140 --> 00:24:57.200
for Kamala Harris, Hillary
Clinton, Elizabeth Warren,

00:24:57.200 --> 00:24:58.900
Bernie Sanders, et
cetera, et cetera.

00:24:58.900 --> 00:25:03.268
So this work was obviously done
during the Democratic primary.

00:25:03.268 --> 00:25:04.060
What do you notice?

00:25:04.060 --> 00:25:08.000
Oh, by the way, all these
ones scattered in here,

00:25:08.000 --> 00:25:09.417
these are random
people taken from

00:25:09.417 --> 00:25:11.250
something called the
face forensic database.

00:25:11.250 --> 00:25:13.430
It was part of a larger
testing that we were doing.

00:25:13.430 --> 00:25:14.770
So you can ignore those.

00:25:14.770 --> 00:25:17.590
But these clumps that you
see here are all individuals.

00:25:17.590 --> 00:25:19.580
Even in this
two-dimensional space--

00:25:19.580 --> 00:25:21.830
this is an, originally,
190-dimensional space--

00:25:21.830 --> 00:25:24.460
you see this very
nice, tight clustering.

00:25:24.460 --> 00:25:27.400
So Kamala Harris, all her
videos cluster together.

00:25:27.400 --> 00:25:33.210
Obama, Arora, Booker, Trump,
Sanders, Warren, et cetera.

00:25:33.210 --> 00:25:34.680
So how do we exploit this?

00:25:34.680 --> 00:25:36.240
So what it tells
us, by the way, is

00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:37.920
that people are
pretty consistent

00:25:37.920 --> 00:25:39.520
and they're distinct
from each other.

00:25:39.520 --> 00:25:42.240
We found this, if you
will, soft biometric.

00:25:42.240 --> 00:25:43.798
And I say soft
because I don't think

00:25:43.798 --> 00:25:45.840
I'm going to distinguish
Kamala Harris from seven

00:25:45.840 --> 00:25:48.630
billion people in the
world, but I don't have to.

00:25:48.630 --> 00:25:51.540
I just have to distinguish
her from an impersonator.

00:25:51.540 --> 00:25:53.550
So here's what
we're going to do.

00:25:53.550 --> 00:25:55.620
We do this in the full
190-dimensional space,

00:25:55.620 --> 00:25:57.240
but we can visualize it here.

00:25:57.240 --> 00:26:00.430
We build a simple, one-class,
non-linear support vector

00:26:00.430 --> 00:26:00.930
machine.

00:26:00.930 --> 00:26:02.760
Nothing fancy.

00:26:02.760 --> 00:26:05.410
No neural nets, nothing
complicated here.

00:26:05.410 --> 00:26:10.470
So we have a bounding surface
around the authentic Obama

00:26:10.470 --> 00:26:11.250
videos.

00:26:11.250 --> 00:26:14.157
And then any 10-second
clip, face track.

00:26:14.157 --> 00:26:15.990
Extract the action units
and head movements,

00:26:15.990 --> 00:26:17.760
190 correlations.

00:26:17.760 --> 00:26:20.352
Put a point into this
space, and are you

00:26:20.352 --> 00:26:21.810
within the bounding
surface or not?

00:26:21.810 --> 00:26:23.160
If yes, authentic.

00:26:23.160 --> 00:26:24.480
If not, not authentic.

00:26:24.480 --> 00:26:26.850
And you notice, by the
way, these data points

00:26:26.850 --> 00:26:28.440
are the deep fake Obama.

00:26:28.440 --> 00:26:30.780
They're close, but
they're outside.

00:26:30.780 --> 00:26:32.970
And if you're
outside of here, it's

00:26:32.970 --> 00:26:34.500
not that you're a deep fake.

00:26:34.500 --> 00:26:35.910
It's just that you're not Obama.

00:26:35.910 --> 00:26:37.620
But that's all I care about.

00:26:37.620 --> 00:26:39.900
If I'm looking at a video
and it looks like Obama,

00:26:39.900 --> 00:26:42.180
I just want to know, are
the behavioral mannerisms,

00:26:42.180 --> 00:26:44.370
are the temporal
dynamics unfolding over

00:26:44.370 --> 00:26:48.780
a 10-second window, consistent
with the mannerisms of Obama

00:26:48.780 --> 00:26:51.870
or Harris or
Clinton, or whomever?

00:26:51.870 --> 00:26:56.240
So let me show you some
large-scale testing.

00:26:56.240 --> 00:26:58.180
So we have a number of
different data sets.

00:26:58.180 --> 00:27:00.400
I'll give you specific
numbers in a minute.

00:27:00.400 --> 00:27:02.310
Original Obama,
super easy to get.

00:27:02.310 --> 00:27:04.150
Just go on YouTube
and scrape YouTube.

00:27:04.150 --> 00:27:07.593
You can get hours and hours
and hours of video of Obama.

00:27:07.593 --> 00:27:09.010
We had some
colleagues and friends

00:27:09.010 --> 00:27:11.680
up at the University of
Washington create some lip sync

00:27:11.680 --> 00:27:13.295
deep fakes of Obama.

00:27:13.295 --> 00:27:14.920
So now just the mouth
has been replaced

00:27:14.920 --> 00:27:16.810
with a new audio track.

00:27:16.810 --> 00:27:19.960
There's a very good impersonator
on YouTube of Obama.

00:27:19.960 --> 00:27:21.170
This is not a deep fake.

00:27:21.170 --> 00:27:22.920
It's just somebody
pretending to be Obama.

00:27:22.920 --> 00:27:24.670
He's very funny.

00:27:24.670 --> 00:27:26.980
From that, our friends at
the University of Southern

00:27:26.980 --> 00:27:28.900
California, Hao
Li and his group,

00:27:28.900 --> 00:27:32.560
created face-swapped deep fakes,
where we swapped Obama's face

00:27:32.560 --> 00:27:35.830
onto the impersonator's video.

00:27:35.830 --> 00:27:36.700
Yeah, they did that.

00:27:36.700 --> 00:27:39.940
And then also, the USC group
made a bunch of puppet master

00:27:39.940 --> 00:27:43.150
deep fakes, where one of the
graduate students in Hao Li's

00:27:43.150 --> 00:27:46.660
lab was just sitting in front
of the camera, animating Obama.

00:27:46.660 --> 00:27:49.028
So we have 1, 2, 3 deep fakes.

00:27:49.028 --> 00:27:51.070
So those are the three
that I enumerated earlier.

00:27:51.070 --> 00:27:53.650
We just have an impersonator,
because if we can detect

00:27:53.650 --> 00:27:55.930
the differences, it doesn't
matter whether it's just

00:27:55.930 --> 00:27:58.120
a real video but it's
somebody who's not Obama,

00:27:58.120 --> 00:27:59.860
or it is, in fact, a deep fake.

00:27:59.860 --> 00:28:02.810
And then, of course, we
have the authentic videos.

00:28:02.810 --> 00:28:05.710
All right, so here's how I'm
going to show you the results.

00:28:05.710 --> 00:28:08.260
I'm going to ask, what is
the ability to distinguish

00:28:08.260 --> 00:28:11.680
authentic Obama videos, of
which there was about 207,000

00:28:11.680 --> 00:28:14.740
10-second clips,
from random people--

00:28:14.740 --> 00:28:16.720
these are primarily
newscasters--

00:28:16.720 --> 00:28:19.210
the comedic impersonator that
I just showed you earlier,

00:28:19.210 --> 00:28:21.763
and the three types
of deep fakes?

00:28:21.763 --> 00:28:23.680
The numbers under each
of these are the number

00:28:23.680 --> 00:28:26.110
of 10-second video
clips that we're

00:28:26.110 --> 00:28:28.150
going to try to categorize.

00:28:28.150 --> 00:28:30.880
Again, I don't have to
learn using this data.

00:28:30.880 --> 00:28:32.650
This is pure testing data.

00:28:32.650 --> 00:28:34.360
I train on this data--

00:28:34.360 --> 00:28:37.210
authentic-- and it's a one-class
support vector machine.

00:28:37.210 --> 00:28:39.850
So anything that falls
out is not Obama,

00:28:39.850 --> 00:28:42.400
whether it's a random person,
a comedic impersonator,

00:28:42.400 --> 00:28:43.840
or a deep fake.

00:28:43.840 --> 00:28:47.080
I'm going to present the
results as area under the curve.

00:28:47.080 --> 00:28:49.150
0.5 means I'm a chance.

00:28:49.150 --> 00:28:51.730
I'm guessing whether
it's real or fake.

00:28:51.730 --> 00:28:55.790
1.0 means I've got a
perfect classifier.

00:28:55.790 --> 00:28:58.270
All right, let's see how we did.

00:28:58.270 --> 00:29:01.360
OK, so for random people,
comedic impersonators,

00:29:01.360 --> 00:29:08.450
face swap, and puppet master,
we're roughly an AUC of 0.95.

00:29:08.450 --> 00:29:09.340
That's pretty good.

00:29:09.340 --> 00:29:12.160
Not perfect, but pretty good--
on a single 10-second clip,

00:29:12.160 --> 00:29:12.820
by the way.

00:29:12.820 --> 00:29:14.020
That's actually not bad.

00:29:14.020 --> 00:29:17.130
That's all I'm working
on, is a 10-second clip.

00:29:17.130 --> 00:29:18.510
We took a big hit here.

00:29:18.510 --> 00:29:21.180
We're at around a little
bit above 0.8 on lip sync.

00:29:21.180 --> 00:29:22.865
Why is that, by the way?

00:29:22.865 --> 00:29:23.740
Does that make sense?

00:29:23.740 --> 00:29:25.260
So first of all, sure.

00:29:25.260 --> 00:29:27.510
What's the nature of a lip
sync deep fake, because all

00:29:27.510 --> 00:29:29.370
I've done is replace the mouth?

00:29:29.370 --> 00:29:31.170
The rest of the head
is authentic, so

00:29:31.170 --> 00:29:34.980
how he's moving his head
and his eyebrows is Obama.

00:29:34.980 --> 00:29:37.840
In fact, most of the mannerisms
here are perfectly fine,

00:29:37.840 --> 00:29:41.460
so it makes sense that
with these deep fakes,

00:29:41.460 --> 00:29:43.710
and for people who are not
Obama, we should do better.

00:29:43.710 --> 00:29:44.790
But here we do worse.

00:29:44.790 --> 00:29:46.290
I won't show you
the slide for that,

00:29:46.290 --> 00:29:48.840
but if we're careful
on the features

00:29:48.840 --> 00:29:50.970
that we use, always
using one action

00:29:50.970 --> 00:29:53.700
unit from the mouth and
another from the head,

00:29:53.700 --> 00:29:56.230
this can bump up to about 0.95.

00:29:56.230 --> 00:29:59.310
So if we're a little careful,
we can get a bump here.

00:29:59.310 --> 00:30:01.560
But we can also get
a bump if we just

00:30:01.560 --> 00:30:03.450
consider that most
videos that we see

00:30:03.450 --> 00:30:05.770
are typically two
to three minutes.

00:30:05.770 --> 00:30:08.700
And so if we just simply
integrate over all

00:30:08.700 --> 00:30:11.790
of the 10-second clips in
a video, just averaging,

00:30:11.790 --> 00:30:15.120
you can see you get this
nice bump in the dark--

00:30:15.120 --> 00:30:17.890
I don't know what color
that is, blue let's call it.

00:30:17.890 --> 00:30:21.180
So here, with the puppet master,
we have just about a 1.0.

00:30:21.180 --> 00:30:23.940
We got a nice bump on the
lip sync to above 0.9.

00:30:23.940 --> 00:30:25.980
And now we're getting
really close to perfect

00:30:25.980 --> 00:30:29.820
classification, distinguishing
only using these behavioral

00:30:29.820 --> 00:30:32.730
mannerisms over an average of
a two- to three-minute video--

00:30:32.730 --> 00:30:35.580
two and a half minute
video, rather--

00:30:35.580 --> 00:30:37.320
of Obama.

00:30:37.320 --> 00:30:40.080
Now, that's for
Obama, of course.

00:30:40.080 --> 00:30:40.800
I will tell you--

00:30:40.800 --> 00:30:42.420
I don't have the slide
for this, apparently--

00:30:42.420 --> 00:30:44.430
is that we've done this
for Hillary Clinton.

00:30:44.430 --> 00:30:45.420
We've done it for Kamala Harris.

00:30:45.420 --> 00:30:46.260
We've done it for Joe Biden.

00:30:46.260 --> 00:30:47.170
We've done it for
Bernie Sanders.

00:30:47.170 --> 00:30:48.180
We've done it for Donald Trump.

00:30:48.180 --> 00:30:50.350
And the accuracies are all
almost exactly the same.

00:30:50.350 --> 00:30:52.750
There's almost no difference,
except one exception.

00:30:52.750 --> 00:30:56.340
We're really, really good at
detecting Elizabeth Warren

00:30:56.340 --> 00:30:59.220
fakes, and the reason is she
is so incredibly animated.

00:30:59.220 --> 00:31:03.210
If you ever seen her talk, you
are all from Massachusetts.

00:31:03.210 --> 00:31:05.700
She is just really,
super animated.

00:31:05.700 --> 00:31:07.870
Nobody can reproduce
that animation,

00:31:07.870 --> 00:31:10.650
so we do really, really
well on Elizabeth Warren,

00:31:10.650 --> 00:31:12.820
slightly better
than everybody else.

00:31:12.820 --> 00:31:14.520
So this generalizes
to various people.

00:31:14.520 --> 00:31:18.130
It's not just an
Obama classifier.

00:31:18.130 --> 00:31:21.070
Now, I'm aware
that we are living

00:31:21.070 --> 00:31:23.350
through a machine
learning revolution,

00:31:23.350 --> 00:31:25.810
or I should say the third
machine learning revolution.

00:31:25.810 --> 00:31:28.180
And I understand that
it's very popular to throw

00:31:28.180 --> 00:31:30.520
machine learning at problems.

00:31:30.520 --> 00:31:32.500
I think it's really
nice to understand

00:31:32.500 --> 00:31:35.440
what it is that we've learned.

00:31:35.440 --> 00:31:37.032
So it's nice that
we can look at this

00:31:37.032 --> 00:31:39.490
and say, look, we've learned
this behavioral mannerism that

00:31:39.490 --> 00:31:42.145
has to do with how
people move, for example,

00:31:42.145 --> 00:31:43.270
their head and their mouth.

00:31:43.270 --> 00:31:45.395
And for each individual,
I can tell you what it is.

00:31:45.395 --> 00:31:47.920
I can go in and look and see
what are the features that

00:31:47.920 --> 00:31:49.440
are most diagnostic.

00:31:49.440 --> 00:31:51.700
So for Elizabeth Warren,
it's the head movement,

00:31:51.700 --> 00:31:52.870
left and right as she talks.

00:31:52.870 --> 00:31:54.460
Of course it is.

00:31:54.460 --> 00:31:56.530
And for Trump, it's
something else.

00:31:56.530 --> 00:31:58.360
And for Obama, it's
something else.

00:31:58.360 --> 00:32:01.180
I'm also aware that we've
handcrafted these features.

00:32:01.180 --> 00:32:02.920
We have, by hand,
selected things

00:32:02.920 --> 00:32:05.140
that is almost
certainly not optimal.

00:32:05.140 --> 00:32:07.170
But learning what
we've learned now,

00:32:07.170 --> 00:32:08.920
let's see if we can
do a little bit better

00:32:08.920 --> 00:32:10.640
by throwing some
machine learning at it.

00:32:10.640 --> 00:32:12.390
So let me tell you a
little bit about what

00:32:12.390 --> 00:32:14.860
we did, following on that
initial investigation, where

00:32:14.860 --> 00:32:17.440
we got this idea
that we could use

00:32:17.440 --> 00:32:20.505
these behavioral mannerisms
to detect impersonators

00:32:20.505 --> 00:32:21.130
and deep fakes.

00:32:21.130 --> 00:32:24.310
So here's what we're going to do
to try to tidy this up a little

00:32:24.310 --> 00:32:28.260
bit and eke out a little
bit more performance.

00:32:28.260 --> 00:32:29.940
We're going to
start with FAb-Net.

00:32:29.940 --> 00:32:32.100
So FAb-Net is a
deep neural network

00:32:32.100 --> 00:32:35.550
that captures the
appearance of people.

00:32:35.550 --> 00:32:37.680
And basically what it does,
it takes a single image

00:32:37.680 --> 00:32:43.380
and it extracts a
256-dimensional vector

00:32:43.380 --> 00:32:46.830
that represents a
person's appearance.

00:32:46.830 --> 00:32:50.130
Now, we care about
time, so here I'm

00:32:50.130 --> 00:32:51.650
going to have T equals 1 to 100.

00:32:51.650 --> 00:32:53.820
So that's 100
frames now, so we've

00:32:53.820 --> 00:32:57.120
reduced it down to about four
seconds from the original 10.

00:32:57.120 --> 00:32:59.520
We did that primarily for
computational considerations,

00:32:59.520 --> 00:33:00.570
because once you
start doing everything

00:33:00.570 --> 00:33:02.737
with neural networks, it
gets a little more tedious.

00:33:02.737 --> 00:33:05.130
So we're now only working on
four-second video clips, 24

00:33:05.130 --> 00:33:07.110
frames a second, 100 frames.

00:33:07.110 --> 00:33:10.230
Each one of these gets
shoved through a FAb-Net.

00:33:10.230 --> 00:33:12.450
What does FAb-Net do?

00:33:12.450 --> 00:33:16.620
This representation right here,
this 256-dimensional vector,

00:33:16.620 --> 00:33:19.200
is a representation of
a person's appearance,

00:33:19.200 --> 00:33:23.220
so that if you do a query
against a database of faces

00:33:23.220 --> 00:33:25.920
using that representation,
you will get things

00:33:25.920 --> 00:33:27.750
that have a similar appearance.

00:33:27.750 --> 00:33:30.710
So for example, here in
the middle row, this woman,

00:33:30.710 --> 00:33:31.918
her lips are slightly pursed.

00:33:31.918 --> 00:33:33.460
She's looking directly
at the camera,

00:33:33.460 --> 00:33:34.650
and her eyes are closed.

00:33:34.650 --> 00:33:38.460
The nearest neighbors
that you see to the right

00:33:38.460 --> 00:33:41.850
here using that
representation are, well,

00:33:41.850 --> 00:33:43.270
people who sort
of look the same.

00:33:43.270 --> 00:33:46.170
But it's not the same
person, most importantly.

00:33:46.170 --> 00:33:48.630
Here, for example, the person
with this slight profile,

00:33:48.630 --> 00:33:51.870
and nearest neighbors are
people similarly profiled

00:33:51.870 --> 00:33:53.310
but different identity.

00:33:53.310 --> 00:33:54.870
That's the whole
point of FAb-Net,

00:33:54.870 --> 00:33:58.380
is that you are capturing some
characteristic that embodies

00:33:58.380 --> 00:34:01.410
the appearance of the image.

00:34:01.410 --> 00:34:03.640
So that's not
exactly what we want,

00:34:03.640 --> 00:34:06.580
but it's a step in the right
direction of what we want.

00:34:06.580 --> 00:34:10.020
So what we do is we take each
frame across four seconds.

00:34:10.020 --> 00:34:11.550
We shove them into
FAb-Net and we

00:34:11.550 --> 00:34:14.650
get this 256-dimensional
representation.

00:34:14.650 --> 00:34:15.810
Now, what do we want to do?

00:34:15.810 --> 00:34:18.210
We want to capture
behavioral mannerisms.

00:34:18.210 --> 00:34:20.790
So we really want time in there.

00:34:20.790 --> 00:34:22.949
So that FAb-Net is one step.

00:34:22.949 --> 00:34:23.639
Why?

00:34:23.639 --> 00:34:26.489
Well, if my head is turned
this way and I'm frowning,

00:34:26.489 --> 00:34:27.690
that's an appearance.

00:34:27.690 --> 00:34:29.400
But then what happens
in the next frame?

00:34:29.400 --> 00:34:30.520
Ah, I start to smile.

00:34:30.520 --> 00:34:34.540
OK, so I want to capture that
change in appearance over time,

00:34:34.540 --> 00:34:36.635
but I also care about identity.

00:34:36.635 --> 00:34:38.010
So here's what
we're going to do.

00:34:38.010 --> 00:34:39.409
Take the 100 frames.

00:34:39.409 --> 00:34:40.659
Each one goes through FAb-Net.

00:34:40.659 --> 00:34:43.530
You get 256-dimensional
representation.

00:34:43.530 --> 00:34:44.860
Pack that into a matrix.

00:34:44.860 --> 00:34:47.489
So now it's 100 by 256.

00:34:47.489 --> 00:34:50.310
That's this step right here.

00:34:50.310 --> 00:34:53.520
We're going to shove that
representation into something

00:34:53.520 --> 00:34:55.530
that we built
called Behavior-Net.

00:34:55.530 --> 00:34:57.390
So that's that little
green box right there.

00:34:57.390 --> 00:35:00.487
And out of that comes
512 dimensional vectors.

00:35:00.487 --> 00:35:02.820
I'm not going to talk about
all the nitty-gritty details

00:35:02.820 --> 00:35:03.330
about this.

00:35:03.330 --> 00:35:05.220
I will give a link at
the end of the talk

00:35:05.220 --> 00:35:06.890
with a paper that
describes this.

00:35:06.890 --> 00:35:08.130
A couple of quick things.

00:35:08.130 --> 00:35:11.370
This is a standard
architecture using ResNet-101.

00:35:11.370 --> 00:35:13.770
We use a metric learning
objective function

00:35:13.770 --> 00:35:15.630
and a VoxCeleb2 data set.

00:35:15.630 --> 00:35:18.540
And what we were trying
to do was the following.

00:35:18.540 --> 00:35:22.170
The objective here was
to do the following.

00:35:22.170 --> 00:35:26.700
Given not just a single
image, but 100 images,

00:35:26.700 --> 00:35:32.100
four-second clip, we wanted
to find similar clips in terms

00:35:32.100 --> 00:35:35.160
of identity and appearance.

00:35:35.160 --> 00:35:40.690
So for example, here, in
that little video clip,

00:35:40.690 --> 00:35:43.240
you can now see The Rock is
moving and this person moving.

00:35:43.240 --> 00:35:45.430
There's 100 frames.

00:35:45.430 --> 00:35:48.783
Out of those 100 frames,
we push him into FAb-Net.

00:35:48.783 --> 00:35:50.200
We push him into
our Behavior-Net,

00:35:50.200 --> 00:35:52.960
and out comes a
512-dimensional representation.

00:35:52.960 --> 00:35:56.050
We then look for the nearest
neighbor in the big data

00:35:56.050 --> 00:35:59.260
set of videos and images that
we have and we extract the most

00:35:59.260 --> 00:36:00.380
similar.

00:36:00.380 --> 00:36:02.770
And now you see something
really interesting.

00:36:02.770 --> 00:36:06.820
You see, first of all, it finds
the same person and the thing

00:36:06.820 --> 00:36:08.660
that they are doing is similar.

00:36:08.660 --> 00:36:11.560
So here's The Rock at
slight profile talking.

00:36:11.560 --> 00:36:12.430
Here's this guy.

00:36:12.430 --> 00:36:14.260
He starts talking and
he turns his head.

00:36:14.260 --> 00:36:16.210
The nearest neighbors
do the same thing.

00:36:16.210 --> 00:36:17.230
Same thing on this one.

00:36:17.230 --> 00:36:19.840
This woman is talking, and then
she turns her head slightly.

00:36:19.840 --> 00:36:22.010
Nearest neighbors
do the same thing.

00:36:22.010 --> 00:36:24.400
So the output of
the Behavior-Net

00:36:24.400 --> 00:36:29.807
is now capturing two things,
identity and behavior.

00:36:29.807 --> 00:36:30.890
Well, that's what we want.

00:36:30.890 --> 00:36:34.240
So instead of hard-wiring
the action units and the head

00:36:34.240 --> 00:36:37.600
movements, we're now going to
use that learned representation

00:36:37.600 --> 00:36:40.630
from Behavior-Net, and I'm
simply using this visualization

00:36:40.630 --> 00:36:42.935
to tell you that that
Behavior-Net seems

00:36:42.935 --> 00:36:44.560
to have learned the
representation that

00:36:44.560 --> 00:36:49.210
captures both identity
and behavior mannerisms.

00:36:49.210 --> 00:36:50.140
All right.

00:36:50.140 --> 00:36:51.820
So that's one pipeline.

00:36:51.820 --> 00:36:53.790
Everybody goes into
FAb-Net in the blue.

00:36:53.790 --> 00:36:56.190
FAb-Net goes into this
Behavior-Net, that little box

00:36:56.190 --> 00:36:56.690
there.

00:36:56.690 --> 00:36:58.570
Could be a whole other talk,
talk about how we did that,

00:36:58.570 --> 00:36:59.770
but I'll ignore it for now.

00:36:59.770 --> 00:37:02.920
A 512 representation, that
captures the underlying

00:37:02.920 --> 00:37:04.453
behavioral mannerisms.

00:37:04.453 --> 00:37:05.995
Let me just describe
another pipeline

00:37:05.995 --> 00:37:08.287
because we're going to combine
a few things in the end.

00:37:08.287 --> 00:37:11.710
Each of these image is then
shoved into a VGG, which

00:37:11.710 --> 00:37:13.300
is a standard face recognition.

00:37:13.300 --> 00:37:16.430
Out of that comes a
496-dimensional representation,

00:37:16.430 --> 00:37:18.280
which you can just use
for face recognition.

00:37:18.280 --> 00:37:19.780
There's nothing
special about VGG.

00:37:19.780 --> 00:37:22.390
We just use some off
the shelf representation

00:37:22.390 --> 00:37:24.160
for face recognition.

00:37:24.160 --> 00:37:26.680
We get 100 of those for each
of the frames of the video.

00:37:26.680 --> 00:37:27.770
We simply do an average.

00:37:27.770 --> 00:37:28.270
Why?

00:37:28.270 --> 00:37:31.030
Well, because we expect the
face to have the same identity.

00:37:31.030 --> 00:37:35.080
So out of this pipeline,
in orange, comes identity--

00:37:35.080 --> 00:37:38.350
spatial, static identity
based on face recognition.

00:37:38.350 --> 00:37:41.650
Out of this pipeline
comes behavioral identity,

00:37:41.650 --> 00:37:45.538
having to do with the
mannerisms evolving over time.

00:37:45.538 --> 00:37:47.080
And now what we want
to do is we have

00:37:47.080 --> 00:37:50.020
a reference set of
authentic videos,

00:37:50.020 --> 00:37:51.430
where we know who it is.

00:37:51.430 --> 00:37:53.020
We know what the mannerisms are.

00:37:53.020 --> 00:37:56.020
And we simply ask a
very simple question.

00:37:56.020 --> 00:37:58.960
For this sequence, there
is an identity match

00:37:58.960 --> 00:38:00.580
based on the face.

00:38:00.580 --> 00:38:03.100
This is Kamala Harris.

00:38:03.100 --> 00:38:05.380
We have a match based on
the behavioral mannerism.

00:38:05.380 --> 00:38:08.170
Who is the closest, in terms
of the mannerism, of this?

00:38:08.170 --> 00:38:10.550
And then we simply ask,
well, is it the same person?

00:38:10.550 --> 00:38:13.120
So does the mannerism
match the face?

00:38:13.120 --> 00:38:15.730
And if the answer is
yes, it's authentic.

00:38:15.730 --> 00:38:17.590
And if the answer
is no, then we say,

00:38:17.590 --> 00:38:20.830
OK, it looks like Kamala
Harris but it's not

00:38:20.830 --> 00:38:22.480
moving like Kamala Harris.

00:38:22.480 --> 00:38:23.440
Mismatch.

00:38:23.440 --> 00:38:24.520
It's a fake.

00:38:24.520 --> 00:38:26.420
So that's what
we're going to do.

00:38:26.420 --> 00:38:28.430
Now, let me just show
you the evaluation.

00:38:28.430 --> 00:38:30.830
So we had a couple of
different data sets.

00:38:30.830 --> 00:38:34.280
What I'm plotting on here
are 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 data sets,

00:38:34.280 --> 00:38:37.340
in terms of the number of
identities in the data set

00:38:37.340 --> 00:38:40.050
and the average
duration of the video.

00:38:40.050 --> 00:38:42.380
This is the data set that
we created that I was just

00:38:42.380 --> 00:38:43.280
talking to you about.

00:38:43.280 --> 00:38:45.613
WLDR stands for world leaders.

00:38:45.613 --> 00:38:47.030
There's only 10
identities, but we

00:38:47.030 --> 00:38:51.670
have 14,000 seconds
per identity,

00:38:51.670 --> 00:38:55.920
so we have really long
sequences per person.

00:38:55.920 --> 00:38:57.410
This is the face
forensic database,

00:38:57.410 --> 00:39:01.580
which has 10,000 people but
only 19 seconds per person.

00:39:01.580 --> 00:39:03.620
And then there's a few
data sets in between

00:39:03.620 --> 00:39:04.870
that are somewhere in between.

00:39:04.870 --> 00:39:08.780
We're going to evaluate the
Behavior-Net that I just

00:39:08.780 --> 00:39:11.020
described across all
of these data sets.

00:39:11.020 --> 00:39:12.770
This time I'm going
to present the results

00:39:12.770 --> 00:39:14.490
in a slightly different way.

00:39:14.490 --> 00:39:18.410
This is the average accuracy
across all five data sets.

00:39:18.410 --> 00:39:21.230
Along here is a parameter,
just a threshold

00:39:21.230 --> 00:39:23.730
that allows us to know when
we should make a decision.

00:39:23.730 --> 00:39:25.290
And this is the percent correct.

00:39:25.290 --> 00:39:27.230
I fear that this might
be a little small.

00:39:27.230 --> 00:39:28.790
100% is at the top.

00:39:28.790 --> 00:39:30.650
0% is at the bottom.

00:39:30.650 --> 00:39:36.690
Crossover point is roughly
97%, across all the data set.

00:39:36.690 --> 00:39:38.660
So you can see
here 97%, 99%, 98%.

00:39:38.660 --> 00:39:39.160
Oops.

00:39:39.160 --> 00:39:40.043
88%.

00:39:40.043 --> 00:39:41.460
I'll get back to
that in a minute.

00:39:41.460 --> 00:39:42.200
And 93%.

00:39:42.200 --> 00:39:45.890
So you can see the crossover
for real in purple and fake

00:39:45.890 --> 00:39:49.640
in green are in the high 90%s.

00:39:49.640 --> 00:39:51.830
I don't have a slide, I'm
sorry, to do a comparison

00:39:51.830 --> 00:39:52.913
to the original technique.

00:39:52.913 --> 00:39:55.520
But I will tell you,
we get a 5% to 10%

00:39:55.520 --> 00:39:58.045
bump, relative to the
handcrafted features.

00:39:58.045 --> 00:40:00.170
Not as much as you would
have expected, by the way.

00:40:00.170 --> 00:40:03.050
I expected the machine learning
would have learned a much more

00:40:03.050 --> 00:40:07.640
diagnostic and
representative feature,

00:40:07.640 --> 00:40:10.160
but in fact, it's a
relatively modest bump

00:40:10.160 --> 00:40:12.180
after all of that work.

00:40:12.180 --> 00:40:14.640
Now, we took a
really big hit here.

00:40:14.640 --> 00:40:16.740
We went from the high
90%s to the high 80%s.

00:40:16.740 --> 00:40:17.638
And why is that?

00:40:17.638 --> 00:40:18.930
What is it about this data set?

00:40:18.930 --> 00:40:20.760
And I like this plot
a lot because it's

00:40:20.760 --> 00:40:23.190
a cautionary tale
when you're using

00:40:23.190 --> 00:40:26.350
these big data sets that you
download from the internet.

00:40:26.350 --> 00:40:30.160
So this is one example from
the data set, the DFDC data

00:40:30.160 --> 00:40:32.430
set, which was built by
the folks at Facebook.

00:40:35.530 --> 00:40:37.860
So these were meant to
be face swap deep fakes.

00:40:37.860 --> 00:40:39.880
This is one frame
of the source video

00:40:39.880 --> 00:40:42.410
and one frame of
the target video,

00:40:42.410 --> 00:40:45.310
and this is the corresponding
frame of the face swap.

00:40:45.310 --> 00:40:49.330
Now, what this should have
been is this person's identity

00:40:49.330 --> 00:40:52.480
mapped onto that
person's mannerisms,

00:40:52.480 --> 00:40:54.410
but that's not what happened.

00:40:54.410 --> 00:40:57.920
All this is is a blurry
version of this person.

00:40:57.920 --> 00:40:59.590
And so this face
swap is, in fact,

00:40:59.590 --> 00:41:01.060
not this person's identity.

00:41:01.060 --> 00:41:04.010
It's still this person's
blurry identity.

00:41:04.010 --> 00:41:05.650
And so, in fact,
this database is

00:41:05.650 --> 00:41:08.560
riddled with some problems,
where the face swaps just

00:41:08.560 --> 00:41:09.465
failed.

00:41:09.465 --> 00:41:11.590
And because there's thousands
and thousands of them

00:41:11.590 --> 00:41:14.210
and the quality control
wasn't done very carefully,

00:41:14.210 --> 00:41:14.990
they're out there.

00:41:14.990 --> 00:41:17.173
And so when you're
evaluating against these,

00:41:17.173 --> 00:41:19.090
you have to go back and
look at your mistakes,

00:41:19.090 --> 00:41:21.507
and it turned out the data set
was actually not very good.

00:41:21.507 --> 00:41:24.110
And if we eliminate all these,
we get back into the high 90%s

00:41:24.110 --> 00:41:24.610
again.

00:41:24.610 --> 00:41:28.140
Just a little bit of
a cautionary tale.

00:41:28.140 --> 00:41:31.285
So that's one technique
that Shruti and I

00:41:31.285 --> 00:41:32.160
have been working on.

00:41:32.160 --> 00:41:36.270
Let me talk a little bit about,
going forward, what's next.

00:41:36.270 --> 00:41:38.010
So what are the
challenges in this space?

00:41:38.010 --> 00:41:39.600
I think everybody
here probably knows

00:41:39.600 --> 00:41:44.130
that really rapid,
startlingly rapid advances

00:41:44.130 --> 00:41:45.310
in deep learning.

00:41:45.310 --> 00:41:47.020
So every three to
four months, frankly,

00:41:47.020 --> 00:41:48.728
every time there's a
conference deadline,

00:41:48.728 --> 00:41:51.480
we see new advances in the
synthesis of fake images,

00:41:51.480 --> 00:41:52.580
fake video, fake audio.

00:41:52.580 --> 00:41:55.580
It's a very, very
fast landscape.

00:41:55.580 --> 00:41:58.910
The problem is not, of course,
just creating fake content.

00:41:58.910 --> 00:42:02.750
It's the fact that that content
can get online and spread

00:42:02.750 --> 00:42:06.540
at a speed that is unprecedented
because of social media.

00:42:06.540 --> 00:42:09.248
So if the problem was just
we can create fake content

00:42:09.248 --> 00:42:10.790
but we have no way
of distributing it

00:42:10.790 --> 00:42:12.740
to the world
instantaneously, well, OK,

00:42:12.740 --> 00:42:14.037
it'd still be a problem.

00:42:14.037 --> 00:42:15.620
But this is the
biggest problem, which

00:42:15.620 --> 00:42:17.390
is at once this
stuff is out there--

00:42:17.390 --> 00:42:20.090
once the nonconsensual porn,
once the misinformation,

00:42:20.090 --> 00:42:21.620
once the fraudulent
content gets out

00:42:21.620 --> 00:42:25.340
there-- it is very, very, very
difficult to rein it back in,

00:42:25.340 --> 00:42:28.220
and that's a huge challenge
for us working in this space.

00:42:28.220 --> 00:42:30.710
We have a particularly
polarized public,

00:42:30.710 --> 00:42:33.230
that when videos
come out, people want

00:42:33.230 --> 00:42:34.730
to believe that they're fake.

00:42:34.730 --> 00:42:36.470
So for example,
when George Floyd

00:42:36.470 --> 00:42:38.990
was killed by a
police officer, people

00:42:38.990 --> 00:42:40.940
claimed that it was a
fake video because they

00:42:40.940 --> 00:42:42.320
didn't want to believe it.

00:42:42.320 --> 00:42:45.440
When the video of
Speaker Pelosi came out,

00:42:45.440 --> 00:42:47.390
purportedly showing
her drunk, people

00:42:47.390 --> 00:42:50.000
wanted to believe that she was
drunk, despite the fact that it

00:42:50.000 --> 00:42:50.810
was fake.

00:42:50.810 --> 00:42:53.000
And so when you have a
highly polarized public,

00:42:53.000 --> 00:42:57.170
you can very easily play into
people's preconceived notions,

00:42:57.170 --> 00:43:01.310
and this stuff works because
people want to believe it.

00:43:01.310 --> 00:43:03.350
The biggest threat
here, I would argue,

00:43:03.350 --> 00:43:06.380
is what is called
the liar's dividend.

00:43:06.380 --> 00:43:10.250
So this is a lovely description
from Danielle Citron

00:43:10.250 --> 00:43:13.970
of what happens when
you enter a world where

00:43:13.970 --> 00:43:19.070
any image, any video, any audio,
any news story can be fake.

00:43:19.070 --> 00:43:20.990
Well, then nothing
has to be real.

00:43:20.990 --> 00:43:22.850
Anybody can simply
dismiss something

00:43:22.850 --> 00:43:24.410
as inconvenient truth.

00:43:24.410 --> 00:43:27.470
Here, by the way, is how
fast the world has changed.

00:43:27.470 --> 00:43:29.900
Rewind with me, if
you will, to 2015.

00:43:29.900 --> 00:43:33.410
Then candidate Trump gets
caught on an Access Hollywood

00:43:33.410 --> 00:43:37.160
tape saying some very, very
bad things about women.

00:43:37.160 --> 00:43:38.660
What did he do?

00:43:38.660 --> 00:43:39.590
He apologized.

00:43:39.590 --> 00:43:41.900
He actually said the
words, I am sorry.

00:43:41.900 --> 00:43:43.260
That was wrong.

00:43:43.260 --> 00:43:46.420
It's not who I am today.

00:43:46.420 --> 00:43:50.367
Two years later,
2017 now, a reporter

00:43:50.367 --> 00:43:51.950
asked about the
Access Hollywood tape.

00:43:51.950 --> 00:43:53.560
You know what he said?

00:43:53.560 --> 00:43:54.490
It's fake.

00:43:54.490 --> 00:43:56.990
One swipe of the hand, gone.

00:43:56.990 --> 00:43:58.960
That's how fast the
landscape has shifted

00:43:58.960 --> 00:44:02.042
and people's ability to say,
you can't believe anything.

00:44:02.042 --> 00:44:04.000
Whether it's the New York
Times because they're

00:44:04.000 --> 00:44:06.083
too liberal, whether it's
Fox News because they're

00:44:06.083 --> 00:44:09.130
too conservative, or whether
the facts simply don't conform

00:44:09.130 --> 00:44:12.250
to your worldview about the
number of deaths of COVID,

00:44:12.250 --> 00:44:14.350
about humanitarian
crises around the world,

00:44:14.350 --> 00:44:17.110
about climate change, about any
of a number of things happening

00:44:17.110 --> 00:44:18.880
in the world, you don't
have to believe it

00:44:18.880 --> 00:44:21.490
if you don't want to because
once everything can be fake,

00:44:21.490 --> 00:44:22.670
nothing has to be real.

00:44:22.670 --> 00:44:25.810
I would argue that is by
far the biggest threat that

00:44:25.810 --> 00:44:27.725
is emerging on the landscape.

00:44:27.725 --> 00:44:30.100
The net-- Ted knows this very
well because Ted and I have

00:44:30.100 --> 00:44:32.392
talked about this many times--
is this is an arms race.

00:44:32.392 --> 00:44:34.892
We do a little bit something,
you do a little bit something,

00:44:34.892 --> 00:44:37.270
and the game keeps going
and we never, never quite

00:44:37.270 --> 00:44:40.100
end this game, like everything
else in cybersecurity.

00:44:40.100 --> 00:44:43.480
So it's both fast-paced
and never-ending.

00:44:43.480 --> 00:44:45.010
What are the interventions?

00:44:45.010 --> 00:44:47.695
Well, I think that the
YouTubes and the Facebooks

00:44:47.695 --> 00:44:49.570
and the TikToks and the
Twitters of the world

00:44:49.570 --> 00:44:51.940
have to start taking more
responsibility for the mess

00:44:51.940 --> 00:44:53.720
that they've created in society.

00:44:53.720 --> 00:44:55.780
They have to stop
standing behind

00:44:55.780 --> 00:44:56.980
"we're simply the platform.

00:44:56.980 --> 00:44:57.670
We are the wire.

00:44:57.670 --> 00:44:59.177
We're not responsible."

00:44:59.177 --> 00:45:01.010
I don't think anybody
believes that anymore.

00:45:01.010 --> 00:45:03.820
We have to find a
balance between dealing

00:45:03.820 --> 00:45:06.070
with the misinformation
apocalypse that is among us

00:45:06.070 --> 00:45:07.630
and having the free
and open internet

00:45:07.630 --> 00:45:10.060
that we all want
to exchange ideas.

00:45:10.060 --> 00:45:12.880
I think as we are talking about
regulation-- which is happening

00:45:12.880 --> 00:45:13.780
here on Capitol Hill.

00:45:13.780 --> 00:45:15.228
It's happening in Brussels.

00:45:15.228 --> 00:45:16.270
It's happening in the UK.

00:45:16.270 --> 00:45:17.687
It's happening all
over the world.

00:45:17.687 --> 00:45:20.350
I would argue that we should
spend more time talking

00:45:20.350 --> 00:45:23.200
about the recommendation
algorithms and less time

00:45:23.200 --> 00:45:24.802
talking about the content.

00:45:24.802 --> 00:45:27.010
The fact is that the reason
we have the problem today

00:45:27.010 --> 00:45:29.170
is not because you can
create fake content,

00:45:29.170 --> 00:45:32.860
not because you can post that,
but because the underlying

00:45:32.860 --> 00:45:36.340
recommendation algorithms
favor the most outrageous,

00:45:36.340 --> 00:45:39.490
divisive, hateful,
conspiratorial content

00:45:39.490 --> 00:45:40.810
because it's good for business.

00:45:40.810 --> 00:45:42.820
Hate and outrage sells.

00:45:42.820 --> 00:45:44.320
It keep people engaged.

00:45:44.320 --> 00:45:46.420
Engagement keeps you clicking.

00:45:46.420 --> 00:45:47.890
Clicking is good for revenue.

00:45:47.890 --> 00:45:50.230
We should be talking about
recommendation algorithms

00:45:50.230 --> 00:45:53.590
more than we should be talking
about the underlying content.

00:45:53.590 --> 00:45:56.110
Obviously, we need to be
talking about technological

00:45:56.110 --> 00:45:57.640
interventions, and
obviously we need

00:45:57.640 --> 00:45:59.223
to be talking about
how do you educate

00:45:59.223 --> 00:46:01.630
the next generation
of young minds

00:46:01.630 --> 00:46:03.460
on how to be better
digital citizens

00:46:03.460 --> 00:46:06.460
and consume trusted
information online.

00:46:06.460 --> 00:46:07.600
This is Shruti Agarwal.

00:46:07.600 --> 00:46:10.817
Really, really wonderful
student, who's done all the

00:46:10.817 --> 00:46:13.150
work that I've described and
many other really beautiful

00:46:13.150 --> 00:46:15.790
things in the general
space of digital forensics.

00:46:15.790 --> 00:46:18.220
Let me just touch on a few
things happening in the lab,

00:46:18.220 --> 00:46:20.470
just to give you a flavor
for some of the other things

00:46:20.470 --> 00:46:22.480
that we are working on.

00:46:22.480 --> 00:46:25.210
Sophie Nightingale
is a former postdoc.

00:46:25.210 --> 00:46:28.630
She just started a job
yesterday, in fact,

00:46:28.630 --> 00:46:32.712
at the University of Lancaster
in the UK as a professor.

00:46:32.712 --> 00:46:35.170
Sophie has been doing some very
nice work over the last two

00:46:35.170 --> 00:46:37.240
years on forensic
identification,

00:46:37.240 --> 00:46:41.313
how we identify people
from crime scenes.

00:46:41.313 --> 00:46:42.730
She wrote a really
beautiful paper

00:46:42.730 --> 00:46:44.938
in the proceedings of the
National Academy of Science

00:46:44.938 --> 00:46:49.300
this year, showing that a
technique used by the FBI

00:46:49.300 --> 00:46:51.520
in actual cases over
the last few years,

00:46:51.520 --> 00:46:55.720
trying to identify people from
the wear and tear on blue jeans

00:46:55.720 --> 00:46:59.050
is highly unreliable, despite
the fact that the courts have

00:46:59.050 --> 00:47:00.070
adopted it.

00:47:00.070 --> 00:47:02.530
And more generally, we
are interested in how

00:47:02.530 --> 00:47:04.900
these various digital
forensic techniques are

00:47:04.900 --> 00:47:08.680
being used to identify people,
whether it's based on things

00:47:08.680 --> 00:47:12.190
like clothing, or as
Neerja is working on,

00:47:12.190 --> 00:47:14.680
even simple things, like
estimating people's height

00:47:14.680 --> 00:47:16.870
and weight from a
single photograph,

00:47:16.870 --> 00:47:19.190
turn out to be
highly unreliable.

00:47:19.190 --> 00:47:20.710
So we have a big
emphasis in the lab

00:47:20.710 --> 00:47:23.020
not just on whether
things are real or fake,

00:47:23.020 --> 00:47:25.480
but on how you can extract
reliable information

00:47:25.480 --> 00:47:28.240
in a forensic setting
to identify individuals.

00:47:28.240 --> 00:47:31.660
One quarter of wrongful
convictions in this country

00:47:31.660 --> 00:47:34.000
are due to flawed
forensic science,

00:47:34.000 --> 00:47:35.920
both physical and digital.

00:47:35.920 --> 00:47:40.000
That field is a bit of a
mess, and Sophie and Neerja

00:47:40.000 --> 00:47:43.102
and I are trying to understand
what the landscape looks like,

00:47:43.102 --> 00:47:45.310
figure out what are the
techniques that are reliable,

00:47:45.310 --> 00:47:49.760
what are not, and how to
make them more reliable.

00:47:49.760 --> 00:47:51.650
Julia Dressel was an
undergraduate with me

00:47:51.650 --> 00:47:53.158
at Dartmouth College.

00:47:53.158 --> 00:47:54.700
And this work is
now a few years old,

00:47:54.700 --> 00:47:56.408
but I wanted to mention
because it's also

00:47:56.408 --> 00:47:58.130
in the general space
of criminal justice.

00:47:58.130 --> 00:48:00.590
Julia did a really beautiful
undergraduate thesis

00:48:00.590 --> 00:48:03.380
in understanding the use
of predictive algorithms

00:48:03.380 --> 00:48:06.920
and predicting recidivism that
are being used in the courts

00:48:06.920 --> 00:48:09.680
today, both here in
the US and abroad,

00:48:09.680 --> 00:48:13.250
and how these algorithms
have a serious racial bias,

00:48:13.250 --> 00:48:17.090
and understanding where
that bias is coming from

00:48:17.090 --> 00:48:19.508
and when and if it can
actually be corrected.

00:48:19.508 --> 00:48:21.800
And there's some really nice
work in a Science Advances

00:48:21.800 --> 00:48:25.590
paper that Julia wrote
a couple of years ago.

00:48:25.590 --> 00:48:27.090
Everything that
I've talked about,

00:48:27.090 --> 00:48:29.690
Shruti's work on the
digital forensics,

00:48:29.690 --> 00:48:33.500
Sophie's work and Neerja's
work on the forensic science,

00:48:33.500 --> 00:48:36.230
and Julia's work on
algorithms can all

00:48:36.230 --> 00:48:40.855
be found at that website,
farid.berkeley.edu.

00:48:40.855 --> 00:48:42.230
And I think with
that, I'll stop.

00:48:42.230 --> 00:48:44.200
Thanks very much.

00:48:44.200 --> 00:48:46.080
Thank you very much, Hany.

00:48:46.080 --> 00:48:47.900
That was really wonderful.

00:48:47.900 --> 00:48:50.500
I'm going to give
you my applauses.

00:48:50.500 --> 00:48:53.080
Lovely.

00:48:53.080 --> 00:48:55.330
So let's see.

00:48:55.330 --> 00:48:59.260
We have a question,
a technical question.

00:48:59.260 --> 00:49:02.870
What do you use for
dimensionality reduction?

00:49:02.870 --> 00:49:04.570
Is it PCA?

00:49:04.570 --> 00:49:06.400
Yeah, we did not use PCA.

00:49:06.400 --> 00:49:08.560
We used the t-SNE.

00:49:08.560 --> 00:49:09.930
OK, t-SNE.

00:49:09.930 --> 00:49:12.610
I don't think, by the way, there
was anything special there.

00:49:12.610 --> 00:49:14.985
I think you could have used
just about anything for that.

00:49:14.985 --> 00:49:18.630
But I think t-SNE gives you
slightly cleaner results.

00:49:18.630 --> 00:49:20.730
Fabulous.

00:49:20.730 --> 00:49:26.790
I would like to ask you a
more conceptual question.

00:49:26.790 --> 00:49:31.020
So how can we imagine a
technological solution

00:49:31.020 --> 00:49:36.450
for dealing with
this whole fakeness?

00:49:36.450 --> 00:49:40.020
We have spellcheckers and
we have fake checkers.

00:49:40.020 --> 00:49:42.070
What would it take to get there?

00:49:42.070 --> 00:49:42.920
Good.

00:49:42.920 --> 00:49:43.920
I'm glad you asked this.

00:49:43.920 --> 00:49:46.290
Let me preface this
answer, Daniella,

00:49:46.290 --> 00:49:49.860
by saying that the technology
I'm about to talk about

00:49:49.860 --> 00:49:53.760
is related to work that I do
with a small startup, in which

00:49:53.760 --> 00:49:55.050
I have a financial interest.

00:49:55.050 --> 00:49:56.430
I have a little bit of
conflict of interest

00:49:56.430 --> 00:49:57.660
in what I'm about
to describe to you,

00:49:57.660 --> 00:49:59.670
and I think it's
important to mention that.

00:49:59.670 --> 00:50:01.890
So everything I've
described is what

00:50:01.890 --> 00:50:04.950
falls under the category
of passive forensics.

00:50:04.950 --> 00:50:05.850
You wait.

00:50:05.850 --> 00:50:07.965
You sit back and you wait
for the video to hit,

00:50:07.965 --> 00:50:10.090
and then you try to figure
out if it's real or not.

00:50:10.090 --> 00:50:11.572
And that works
reasonably well when

00:50:11.572 --> 00:50:13.530
it's the New York Times
or the Associated Press

00:50:13.530 --> 00:50:15.570
or Reuters that
are willing to wait

00:50:15.570 --> 00:50:17.010
days or weeks to get it right.

00:50:17.010 --> 00:50:19.140
Or it works well in
the courts of law,

00:50:19.140 --> 00:50:21.690
where you have days, weeks,
months, years to get it right.

00:50:21.690 --> 00:50:24.250
But it doesn't work well on
Twitter, Facebook, YouTube,

00:50:24.250 --> 00:50:26.700
and TikTok, where things
move at light speed.

00:50:26.700 --> 00:50:28.380
So how do you get to
what you just said?

00:50:28.380 --> 00:50:30.480
I like that analogy, by
the way, the spellchecker.

00:50:30.480 --> 00:50:33.010
That's exactly the right
way to think about it.

00:50:33.010 --> 00:50:34.470
So the passive
forensics are never

00:50:34.470 --> 00:50:35.887
going to get there,
in my opinion.

00:50:35.887 --> 00:50:38.610
We're never going to have
the accuracy and the speed

00:50:38.610 --> 00:50:41.550
to be able to analyze any type
of image, any type of video,

00:50:41.550 --> 00:50:43.218
or any type of audio at scale.

00:50:43.218 --> 00:50:44.260
It's not going to happen.

00:50:44.260 --> 00:50:45.660
So what can you do?

00:50:45.660 --> 00:50:49.560
So the work that is being
done that I think can scale

00:50:49.560 --> 00:50:52.200
is so-called control
capture imagery.

00:50:52.200 --> 00:50:53.290
So here's the basic idea.

00:50:53.290 --> 00:50:54.570
It's quite simple.

00:50:54.570 --> 00:50:57.510
On this device, at
the point of capture,

00:50:57.510 --> 00:51:00.990
either in hardware software,
you reach into the image

00:51:00.990 --> 00:51:02.580
and you grab all the pixels.

00:51:02.580 --> 00:51:06.000
You grab the GPS tag and
you grab the daytime tag

00:51:06.000 --> 00:51:08.070
and you cryptographically
sign it.

00:51:08.070 --> 00:51:09.555
You take that
signature and either

00:51:09.555 --> 00:51:11.430
you just attach it to
the image or you put it

00:51:11.430 --> 00:51:14.020
on to a blockchain
type of thing.

00:51:14.020 --> 00:51:16.180
And then any point
in the future,

00:51:16.180 --> 00:51:20.280
you can authenticate the
image back to the original.

00:51:20.280 --> 00:51:21.673
So it's not a watermark.

00:51:21.673 --> 00:51:24.090
We're not inserting something
into the image, which we all

00:51:24.090 --> 00:51:27.510
know has problems because
those can be removed,

00:51:27.510 --> 00:51:28.980
but it's a
cryptographic signature.

00:51:28.980 --> 00:51:31.360
If somebody wants to remove
the signature, it's fine.

00:51:31.360 --> 00:51:33.580
You just won't be able to
authenticate the image.

00:51:33.580 --> 00:51:35.610
I think that's
one technique that

00:51:35.610 --> 00:51:39.870
could actually work at scale if
it was on every single device.

00:51:39.870 --> 00:51:41.370
And the good news
about these things

00:51:41.370 --> 00:51:45.345
is this is where, what, 99.9%
of photos are taken today?

00:51:45.345 --> 00:51:47.220
We're all taking photos
on these devices now.

00:51:47.220 --> 00:51:48.300
They're all HD cameras.

00:51:48.300 --> 00:51:51.130
I bet Ted is probably
taking photos on these.

00:51:51.130 --> 00:51:55.560
So I think that technology
can scale and it can work.

00:51:55.560 --> 00:51:57.437
Now, there are
limitations to that.

00:51:57.437 --> 00:51:58.770
There are things to worry about.

00:51:58.770 --> 00:52:00.820
There are privacy
issues to worry about.

00:52:00.820 --> 00:52:03.657
But if you think about the
direction, it makes sense.

00:52:03.657 --> 00:52:05.490
It doesn't make sense
for me to take a photo

00:52:05.490 --> 00:52:07.240
and send it to the
world and ask everybody

00:52:07.240 --> 00:52:08.880
receiving it to fact-check it.

00:52:08.880 --> 00:52:10.050
That's insane.

00:52:10.050 --> 00:52:12.690
What makes more sense is
for me to take a photo,

00:52:12.690 --> 00:52:15.480
authenticate it, and then
put it out to the world.

00:52:15.480 --> 00:52:18.288
So that is the technology
that I think could work.

00:52:18.288 --> 00:52:19.330
But now what do you need?

00:52:19.330 --> 00:52:22.645
You need Apple and Google to
build it into the devices.

00:52:22.645 --> 00:52:24.270
You need the Facebooks
and the YouTubes

00:52:24.270 --> 00:52:26.010
and the TikToks and the
Twitters and the social medias

00:52:26.010 --> 00:52:28.080
of the world to
prioritize that content,

00:52:28.080 --> 00:52:31.740
to say we will prioritize
trusted content

00:52:31.740 --> 00:52:33.490
over non-trusted content.

00:52:33.490 --> 00:52:36.090
And then you have to deal with
a couple of very tricky issues,

00:52:36.090 --> 00:52:37.860
which is Human Rights Watch.

00:52:37.860 --> 00:52:39.900
What do you do with an
investigative journalist,

00:52:39.900 --> 00:52:41.317
a whistleblower,
or somebody who's

00:52:41.317 --> 00:52:44.730
doing human rights to make
sure that they are not somehow

00:52:44.730 --> 00:52:45.500
being exposed?

00:52:45.500 --> 00:52:47.880
And you've got to figure out
how to add in some anonymity

00:52:47.880 --> 00:52:48.720
to that.

00:52:48.720 --> 00:52:51.173
That technology exists
today, but there's

00:52:51.173 --> 00:52:52.590
a lot of infrastructure
that would

00:52:52.590 --> 00:52:54.330
have to get built around it.

00:52:54.330 --> 00:52:55.080
Thank you, Hany.

00:52:55.080 --> 00:52:56.970
We have just a few more minutes.

00:52:56.970 --> 00:53:00.490
I want to sneak in two more
questions from the audience.

00:53:00.490 --> 00:53:02.910
Can you generally tell
if a picture is fake just

00:53:02.910 --> 00:53:06.930
by trying to identify
pixel overlays?

00:53:06.930 --> 00:53:08.640
By identifying pixel overlays?

00:53:08.640 --> 00:53:10.840
I'm not sure exactly
what that means.

00:53:10.840 --> 00:53:13.050
So let me answer it this way.

00:53:13.050 --> 00:53:14.970
We have a number of
different techniques

00:53:14.970 --> 00:53:19.980
for detecting image manipulation
that work at that pixel level,

00:53:19.980 --> 00:53:21.660
that look at things
like, has this

00:53:21.660 --> 00:53:23.970
been compressed more than once?

00:53:23.970 --> 00:53:26.790
Is the color filter
ray bear pattern

00:53:26.790 --> 00:53:29.700
consistent across the image?

00:53:29.700 --> 00:53:33.330
Do you see inconsistencies in
the underlying image noise,

00:53:33.330 --> 00:53:34.550
for example?

00:53:34.550 --> 00:53:37.800
So the answer to that is
sometimes yes, sometimes no.

00:53:37.800 --> 00:53:40.530
The problem with those
really low level, pixel level

00:53:40.530 --> 00:53:42.150
detection schemes
is that if somebody

00:53:42.150 --> 00:53:46.080
takes an image, HD, manipulates
it, and then downsizes it

00:53:46.080 --> 00:53:48.120
by a factor of eight
and compresses it

00:53:48.120 --> 00:53:51.990
to 75% JPEG compression,
all of those artifacts

00:53:51.990 --> 00:53:54.540
disappear and you introduce
all these gnarly JPEG

00:53:54.540 --> 00:53:58.310
artifacts, which make those
very difficult to detect.

00:53:58.310 --> 00:54:00.350
Hany, one last question.

00:54:00.350 --> 00:54:02.390
Do you have any
thoughts about detecting

00:54:02.390 --> 00:54:06.050
fake audio or fake speech to
complement the fake video?

00:54:06.050 --> 00:54:06.560
Yeah.

00:54:06.560 --> 00:54:09.080
We've done some
work on fake audio

00:54:09.080 --> 00:54:11.390
with one of my former PhD
students, Wei Lu, who's

00:54:11.390 --> 00:54:14.660
now at SUNY Buffalo.

00:54:14.660 --> 00:54:15.292
I have to say--

00:54:15.292 --> 00:54:16.500
I'm embarrassed to say this--

00:54:16.500 --> 00:54:18.583
I always thought that when
I went to go do speech,

00:54:18.583 --> 00:54:21.260
it'd be really easy, because
video seems really hard

00:54:21.260 --> 00:54:23.300
and image seems hard,
but not as hard as video.

00:54:23.300 --> 00:54:24.765
And I just figured,
as I moved down

00:54:24.765 --> 00:54:26.390
the dimensionality
it would get easier,

00:54:26.390 --> 00:54:28.730
but audio is
really, really hard.

00:54:28.730 --> 00:54:30.170
We have developed
some techniques.

00:54:30.170 --> 00:54:32.120
I think it's somewhat
of an orphan field.

00:54:32.120 --> 00:54:34.430
Most of the people in my
field don't look at audio,

00:54:34.430 --> 00:54:37.160
and I think it's really
ripe for a lot of really

00:54:37.160 --> 00:54:38.165
interesting questions.

00:54:38.165 --> 00:54:39.290
So we've done a little bit.

00:54:39.290 --> 00:54:41.138
There's a few papers
on my website about it.

00:54:41.138 --> 00:54:42.680
There's a little
bit of a literature,

00:54:42.680 --> 00:54:47.270
but it's a little
neglected, in my opinion.

00:54:47.270 --> 00:54:49.730
Hany, that was really wonderful.

00:54:49.730 --> 00:54:51.410
First of all, great to see you.

00:54:51.410 --> 00:54:57.560
Second, I was so excited to see
the really insightful research

00:54:57.560 --> 00:54:58.230
from your group.

00:54:58.230 --> 00:55:01.190
Thirdly, I look forward
to a better world

00:55:01.190 --> 00:55:07.970
where I will have the
fake catching system

00:55:07.970 --> 00:55:13.010
so I won't have to worry about
fake images and fake videos.

00:55:13.010 --> 00:55:14.480
Really, thank you very much.

00:55:14.480 --> 00:55:17.330
On behalf of all of us,
I'm going to give you

00:55:17.330 --> 00:55:20.690
another round of applause.

00:55:20.690 --> 00:55:25.680
And maybe you can stay
on for a few minutes.

00:55:25.680 --> 00:55:26.225
OK.

00:55:26.225 --> 00:55:27.020
Bye, everyone.

00:55:27.020 --> 00:55:28.380
See you next week.

00:55:28.380 --> 00:55:29.800
Thanks, everybody.

